WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:01.073
Instructor: Okay so,

00:01.073 --> 00:03.570
now that we know what repositories are in general,

00:03.570 --> 00:07.110
let's learn how we instantiate a new repo using git

00:07.110 --> 00:09.000
and before I show you that command,

00:09.000 --> 00:11.970
I'm actually gonna show you a separate, very simple command

00:11.970 --> 00:13.590
called git status.

00:13.590 --> 00:16.740
Git status will come up again and again in this course

00:16.740 --> 00:19.980
and just in your life if you use git.

00:19.980 --> 00:21.060
It's a harmless command.

00:21.060 --> 00:22.380
It just reports back to you

00:22.380 --> 00:25.440
the status in your current repository.

00:25.440 --> 00:28.350
The thing is, we don't have any repositories yet,

00:28.350 --> 00:31.290
or at least if you're following along, we haven't made any.

00:31.290 --> 00:34.920
So if I run git status anywhere on my machine,

00:34.920 --> 00:36.723
assuming I don't have any repos,

00:38.220 --> 00:41.790
I get this message, fatal, not a git repository.

00:41.790 --> 00:44.520
It's telling me, you're not in a git repo.

00:44.520 --> 00:47.190
So it's aware of what git is.

00:47.190 --> 00:50.010
It's not giving me like you know, command not found.

00:50.010 --> 00:54.180
It's telling me, yeah I know, but you're not in a repo.

00:54.180 --> 00:59.180
Versus if I am in a git repo and I run git status,

00:59.250 --> 01:00.840
we see something different.

01:00.840 --> 01:04.590
Now what we see varies and that's why we use git status.

01:04.590 --> 01:06.300
It reports back to us

01:06.300 --> 01:09.690
and gives us information on the current repo we're in.

01:09.690 --> 01:11.280
So we'll use it all the time,

01:11.280 --> 01:14.070
but for now it's just proof that we're not in a repo.

01:14.070 --> 01:15.720
But now we're gonna learn our second command

01:15.720 --> 01:17.610
which is git init.

01:17.610 --> 01:20.280
So this will initialize a new repository

01:20.280 --> 01:23.100
wherever we are in our terminal.

01:23.100 --> 01:25.500
So whatever directory I'm inside of

01:25.500 --> 01:28.950
will become the home of a new git repository.

01:28.950 --> 01:31.800
So it's the equivalent of what this slide showed,

01:31.800 --> 01:34.110
or these diagrams where we have some folder

01:34.110 --> 01:37.470
and we initialize a repo inside of those folders.

01:37.470 --> 01:39.300
We navigate to the folder.

01:39.300 --> 01:42.870
So in my case, I have an empty folder called Basics.

01:42.870 --> 01:43.703
I'll have, you know

01:43.703 --> 01:45.990
different folders for different parts of the course.

01:45.990 --> 01:47.850
I'm gonna make a new directory.

01:47.850 --> 01:49.470
Let's say I'm writing a novel,

01:49.470 --> 01:52.630
so I'm gonna go with MyFirstNovel

01:53.640 --> 01:56.850
and then I'm gonna navigate into there.

01:56.850 --> 01:59.790
This is where I want my git repository.

01:59.790 --> 02:02.250
I want to have a new repo in MyFirstNovel

02:02.250 --> 02:03.630
so I can track the history,

02:03.630 --> 02:05.700
you know, the drafts, the changes I make,

02:05.700 --> 02:08.820
all of that stuff inside of this project

02:08.820 --> 02:11.100
and then I'm gonna run git init.

02:11.100 --> 02:13.410
Before that, just to remind you,

02:13.410 --> 02:15.540
currently not a git repository

02:15.540 --> 02:17.820
when I ran that command, git status.

02:17.820 --> 02:20.340
Now I run git init

02:20.340 --> 02:24.330
and it tells me it initialized an empty git repository n

02:24.330 --> 02:26.550
and then it gives me the whole path to where I am.

02:26.550 --> 02:27.383
MyFirstNovel.

02:28.230 --> 02:30.210
Now, I don't see anything here.

02:30.210 --> 02:33.150
We'll talk about what actually happens in the next video.

02:33.150 --> 02:34.440
There is something here,

02:34.440 --> 02:37.110
it's hidden and it's where git is stored.

02:37.110 --> 02:40.860
But before that, I just wanna recap what we've seen so far.

02:40.860 --> 02:42.330
The command, git init

02:42.330 --> 02:45.480
will instantiate a new git repository for us,

02:45.480 --> 02:49.200
wherever we are, whatever directory we're currently in,

02:49.200 --> 02:52.020
and our terminal where we run that command.

02:52.020 --> 02:54.930
Also, this is something we run one time per project,

02:54.930 --> 02:57.720
so we're not constantly initializing you know,

02:57.720 --> 02:59.910
if we're working on the same project day after day,

02:59.910 --> 03:01.320
if I'm working on this novel

03:01.320 --> 03:03.330
for a couple of months or years,

03:03.330 --> 03:05.703
if more like my speed, probably years,

03:06.810 --> 03:09.390
we would run git init one time.

03:09.390 --> 03:13.050
Git init just enables git in this one project

03:13.050 --> 03:16.050
and then we just use that same repository day after day.

03:16.050 --> 03:17.100
That's kind of the point.

03:17.100 --> 03:19.200
We want that continued history.

03:19.200 --> 03:24.200
Anyway, now if I run git status, we see something different.

03:24.600 --> 03:27.330
So before, not in a repository,

03:27.330 --> 03:29.580
then we initialize git init,

03:29.580 --> 03:31.890
now git status gives me something else.

03:31.890 --> 03:33.120
There's a lot of stuff in here

03:33.120 --> 03:34.980
we don't know about it this point in time,

03:34.980 --> 03:37.530
but we'll make sense of it soon,

03:37.530 --> 03:38.850
but it's just proof

03:38.850 --> 03:41.910
that something git-ish is happening, right?

03:41.910 --> 03:44.850
Instead of this message, not a git repo.

03:44.850 --> 03:46.590
Okay, next up we're gonna talk about

03:46.590 --> 03:49.140
what actually happens when I run git init.

03:49.140 --> 03:51.600
Where is the git folder or files

03:51.600 --> 03:53.190
or what information does it create?

03:53.190 --> 03:54.240
I don't see anything.
